description:
SPLIT OUT OF bd-2z0.3.6 (2026-07-13). That bead's core claim — offspring inherit and mutate their parent's genome — is now PROVEN by a matched pair (mutation off -> child genome bit-identical to parent, compared via to_bits(); mutation on -> child provably differs). What remains unproven is the PROVENANCE half of its acceptance criteria.

WHY PROVENANCE, SEPARATELY. Knowing the child inherited *a* genome is not the same as knowing WHICH parents it came from, whether it was a clone or a crossover, and what the child's evaluator-state policy was. Without that, a phylogeny (bd-16g.3) can draw the edges but cannot say what happened along them, and the genome browser (bd-16g.13) cannot show a parent-child mutation diff at all — it would have nothing to diff against.

THE HARNESS ALREADY EXISTS. bd-2z0.3.2 landed BrainGenomeEnvelope, BrainGenomeMaterial, BrainProvenance, BrainFamilyId, and OffspringStatePolicy. They are simply not asserted END-TO-END THROUGH THE SPAWN PATH. The types being present is not the same as the spawn path filling them in correctly — that is exactly the gap that let heredity itself go unproven for so long ("it compiles" is not evidence).

SCOPE. Assert, through a real reproduction (not a unit test of the types in isolation):
  - the child's BrainProvenance names BOTH parent UIDs for a crossover and ONE for a clone;
  - the provenance distinguishes clone from crossover from mutation-only;
  - genome hashes are recorded and actually differ when the genome differs (a hash that never changes is a hash that proves nothing);
  - OffspringStatePolicy is honoured: a family declaring Reset gets a reset evaluator state, and one declaring Inherit gets the parent's — and the test must FAIL if the policy is ignored, which means testing BOTH policies, not just the default;
  - a MULTI-GENERATION fixture (>= 5 generations) proves genomes keep evolving rather than converging on a placeholder. Assert the generation-N genome differs from generation-0 AND that each generation's provenance chains correctly to the last.

WHY MULTI-GENERATION SPECIFICALLY: a single-generation test cannot distinguish "inheritance works" from "inheritance works ONCE and then something resets". The regression that matters is silent and cumulative.

HARD REQUIREMENT. The tests must refuse to pass vacuously — if the fixture never actually reproduces, fail loudly with a message saying so, rather than asserting over an empty population. (bd-2z0.3.6's guard fired three times during that work: reproduction is DISABLED outright when reproduction_energy_threshold <= 0.0, and is separately gated by reproduction_attempt_interval/chance. A test that silently ran zero reproductions would have "passed".)
